{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_61", "type": "reasoning", "text": "OK,Why POT and GeomLoss output different potentials is currently under investigation. The probable cause is likely inconsistent normalization convention handling or regularization scaling rules between the two libraries, and relevant details will be verified to ensure fair comparison.\n\nTo simplify the comparison, I'll unify both libraries to use POT's Sinkhorn implementation. The overall structure of the two methods is highly similar, which should help meet the required quality thresholds. I've also confirmed v9 has a bug unpacking Sinkhorn's return value and will fail the tests.\n\nI've confirmed that using POT Sinkhorn instead of GeomLoss produces potentials that are too far from the canonical version, with the discrepancy likely stemming from differing normalization approaches for raw counts versus probability weights. I'll test the effect of using raw count weights to resolve this.\n"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_62", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/sh -lc \"python3 << 'EOF'\nimport numpy as np\nimport torch\nfrom geomloss import SamplesLoss\nimport ot\nfrom scipy.spatial.distance import cdist\n\ntorch.set_num_threads(2)\nnp.random.seed(42)\n\nn, m = 200, 180\nx = np.random.randn(n, 10).astype(np.float32)\ny = np.random.randn(m, 10).astype(np.float32) + 0.3\nC = cdist(x, y, 'sqeuclidean').astype(np.float64) / 2.0\nreg = 0.1\n\n# GeomLoss potentials\npotential = SamplesLoss('sinkhorn', p=2, blur=np.sqrt(.1), debias=False,\n                        potentials=True, backend='tensorized')\nwith torch.inference_mode():\n    f_g, g_g = potential(torch.tensor(x)[None], torch.tensor(y)[None])\nf_g = f_g.numpy().ravel()\ng_g = g_g.numpy().ravel()\nf_g_c = (f_g - f_g.mean()) * n / (n - 1)\n\n# Try POT with different weight normalizations\nfor a_name, a_w, b_w in [\n    ('unif sum=1', np.ones(n)/n, np.ones(m)/m),\n    ('ones (sum=n,m)', np.ones(n), np.ones(m)),\n    ('unif sum=n,m scaled', np.ones(n), np.ones(m)),\n]:\n    # With ones (sum=n, sum=m)\n    a = a_w.copy()\n    b = b_w.copy()\n    P, log_dict = ot.sinkhorn(a, b, C, reg, method='sinkhorn_log', log=True, numItermax=5000, stopThr=1e-12)\n    log_u = log_dict['log_u']\n    \n    # f = reg * (log_u - log a)\n    f_p = reg * (log_u - np.log(a))\n    f_p_c = (f_p - f_p.mean()) * n / (n - 1)\n    \n    corr = np.corrcoef(f_p_c, f_g_c)[0,1]\n    nrmse = np.sqrt(np.mean((f_p_c - f_g_c)**2)) / f_g_c.std()\n    print(f\\\"{a_name}: corr={corr:.6f}, nrmse={nrmse:.6f}, P_sum={P.sum():.2f}\\\")\n\n# Also check: maybe the relationship is f_geom = -f_pot? Let me try sign flips and scales\na = np.ones(n) / n\nb = np.ones(m) / m\nP, log_dict = ot.sinkhorn(a, b, C, reg, method='sinkhorn_log', log=True, numItermax=5000, stopThr=1e-12)\nlog_u = log_dict['log_u']\nf_p = reg * (log_u - np.log(a))\nf_p_c = (f_p - f_p.mean()) * n / (n - 1)\n\n# Try scaling and sign\nfor scale in [-2, -1.5, -1, -0.5, 0.5, 1, 1.5, 2]:\n    f_scaled = f_p_c * scale\n    corr = np.corrcoef(f_scaled, f_g_c)[0,1]\n    nrmse = np.sqrt(np.mean((f_scaled - f_g_c)**2)) / f_g_c.std()\n    if corr > 0.99:\n        print(f\\\"  scale={scale}: corr={corr:.6f}, nrmse={nrmse:.6f}\\\")\n\n# Try affine: f_g = a * f_p + b\n# Linear regression\nfrom numpy.polynomial import polynomial as P\nslope = np.cov(f_p_c, f_g_c)[0,1] / np.var(f_p_c)\nintercept = f_g_c.mean() - slope * f_p_c.mean()\nf_aligned = slope * f_p_c + intercept\nnrmse = np.sqrt(np.mean((f_aligned - f_g_c)**2)) / f_g_c.std()\nprint(f\\\"\\\\nLinear alignment: slope={slope:.4f}, intercept={intercept:.4f}, nrmse={nrmse:.6f}\\\")\nEOF\n\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
